{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And his sister stood afar off.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this is Miriam, for Miriam prophesied that her mother was destined to bear a son who would deliver Israel; and this is what it says, 'to know what would be done to him' — to know what would be the outcome of her prophecy. By way of the Midrash: 'And his sister stood' — this is the Shekhinah. And Scripture teaches you that when the righteous suffer, the Shekhinah presents itself to him to deliver him, in the manner of what is written (Jonah 2:3): ״קראתי מצרה לי אל ה' ויענני״. And as soon as Moses was placed among the reeds, the Shekhinah that was destined to give the Torah through him immediately presented itself to him. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: 'And his sister stood afar off' — this entire verse is said on account of the Shekhinah. 'And she stood' — as written (I Samuel 3:10): ״ויבא ה' ויתיצב״. 'His sister' — as written (Proverbs 7:4): ״אמור לחכמה אחותי את״. 'Afar off' — as written (Jeremiah 31:3): ״מרחוק ה' נראה לי״. 'To know' — as written (I Samuel 2:3): ״כי אל דעות ה'״. 'What' — as written (Deuteronomy 10:12): ״מה ה' אלהיך שואל מעמך״. 'Would be done' — as written (II Samuel 19:14): ״כה יעשה לי אלהים״. 'To him' — as written (Judges 6:24): ״ויקרא לו ה' שלום״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The first citation after 'And his sister stood' is referred to the opening words of the verse (ותתצב), not to 'his sister' specifically; the citation order follows each component of Exodus 2:4 sequentially. Citation for 'to know' given as שמואל א ב — normalized to I Samuel 2:3, the verse containing the phrase כי אל דעות ה'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:14.785273628Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}